# Bench claims — what the logs prove, and nothing past it

Written after a session on 2026-08-30 that produced three confident physical diagnoses in a
row, each rejected by the operator, none of them measured. The firmware side of this project
has strict citation discipline (rule 20) and the bench side had none. This closes that gap:
**a claim about hardware needs the same standard as a constant in the source.**

## Separate the reading from the conclusion

A log line is evidence. What it implies about the physical world is a hypothesis, and it must
be labelled as one until something measures it.

| Say this | Not this |
|---|---|
| "`CDBGPWRUPACK` times out while `DPIDR` reads. That handshake needs die power, so the most likely cause is no core power — a meter on 3V3 settles it." | "The chip is not powered." |
| "No `239A` device and no USB kernel events." | "The board is dead." |
| "The RK900 field is absent, so the read returned null." | "The wind sensor is broken." |

State the observation first, then the inference, then the test that would decide it. When the
operator rejects a hypothesis, **drop it** — do not restate it in new words next turn.

## Two hypotheses is the limit

After two physical explanations have been offered and rejected, stop proposing a third. Say
what is measured, say what is unresolved, and ask what the operator is seeing. They are looking
at the bench; the agent is looking at a log with no ground truth in it.

This is rule 00's bounded-retry rule applied to explanations rather than commands. A third
guess is not diagnosis, it is noise, and it burns the operator's patience at exactly the moment
their information is most needed.

## Never assume what is connected

Do not infer from a successful flash that sensors are attached, that a harness is wired, or
that a pack is present. Nobody said so. Ask, or scope the claim to what was actually observed.

Running a "sensor check" against a board with no sensors on it produces a null result that
means nothing and reads as competence. It is worse than not running it.

## Evidence must name the core

`docs/EVIDENCE.md` entries are worthless if a board was swapped and nothing records which
physical part produced the result. **Capture a core identity in the same breath as the result**,
and put it in the entry:

```bash
# unique per die, survives reflash; exposed as the USB serial number
ioreg -p IOUSB -w0 -l | grep -A20 'idVendor. = 9114' | grep kUSBSerialNumberString
# or over SWD, FICR.DEVICEID
mdw 0x10000060 2
```

"The replacement core" is not an identifier. If no identity was captured, the entry must say so
explicitly rather than implying continuity that was never established.

## Do not restate the obvious

An image built with the A1 flag uses the A1 pin. A sleeping node is asleep. Saying so as though
it were a finding wastes the reader's time and insults them. Before writing a consequence, ask
whether it follows trivially from the name of the thing — if it does, delete it.

See `01-response-style.mdc`; this is the same rule, in the place where it keeps getting broken.
